WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.020
"Can Vitamin E or Selenium Supplements
Prevent or Treat Alzheimer's?"

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.420
Oxidative stress
from free radicals

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.680
is the one of the pathways

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.560
implicated in the development
and deterioration

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.360
of Alzheimer's brains,

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.620
given the evidence of excess
oxidative DNA damage on autopsy.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.800
So, what about trying
an antioxidant?

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.080
Vitamin E levels
tend to be lower

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.360
in the bloodstreams of those
with cognitive impairment

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.880
or dementia,
but which came first?

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:33.240
Maybe Alzheimer's disease
led to poor eating,

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.740
rather than poor eating

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.120
leading to Alzheimer's disease.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.000
Greater vitamin E intake
from foods

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.100
is clearly associated
with a lower risk

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.240
of developing Alzheimer's,

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:46.020
but greater vitamin E intake
from supplements is not.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.480
But, that didn't stop
researchers

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.100
from including a vitamin E arm

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.140
in the largest primary
prevention study

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.820
of Alzheimer's disease to date.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.520
The Prevention
of Alzheimer's Disease

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.620
by Vitamin E and Selenium trial

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.140
randomized more than
7500 older men

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:05.580
to take vitamin E, selenium,
both, or neither-just placebos-

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:06.600
for five years.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.760
To quote from the conclusion:

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:12.000
"Neither supplement
prevented dementia."

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.320
A separate long-term vitamin E
supplementation trial

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.840
of older women similarly found
no protection

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.460
against cognitive decline.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.340
Neither did vitamin E
appear to benefit those

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:24.960
with mild cognitive impairment,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.320
but the trials on Alzheimer's
patients themselves

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.300
started to get more interesting.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.620
The first trial,

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.660
published in the New England
Journal of Medicine,

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.660
randomized more than 150
Alzheimer's patients

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:40.260
to vitamin E or placebo
to see if vitamin E could slow

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:41.880
the progression of the disease.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.520
The primary outcome
was the number of days

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.620
until of one of any
of the following happened:

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.840
death, institutionalization,

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.900
loss of the ability
to perform basic activities

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:52.680
of daily living,

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.320
or sliding into severe dementia,

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:56.940
where you suffer
a complete loss

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.080
of sense of time or place.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:02.520
On first analysis,
vitamin E flopped:

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.560
no significant slowing
of progression.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.220
Digging deeper,
the researchers realized

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.140
that despite
the random allotment,

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.540
the placebo group
just by chance

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.420
ended up with patients
with milder disease.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.700
After taking that into account,

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.320
the difference in outcomes

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:21.060
between the vitamin E
and placebo groups

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.440
widened substantially.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:26.340
Now, this kind of
unplanned post-hoc analysis

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.440
is heavily frowned upon,

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.780
but the effect
seemed so sizable

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.280
they went ahead
and published it.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:33.720
In the placebo group,

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.360
the adjusted time to death
or loss of independence

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:40.020
was 440 days,
but in the vitamin E group,

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.720
after the baseline cognition
was normalized,

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.200
it was 670 days,
more than seven months later.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.800
However,
given the unorthodox analysis,

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:51.600
the scientific world waited

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.160
for the study to be replicated.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.880
And here we go:
more than 600 patients

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:58.860
with mild to moderate
Alzheimer's disease

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.260
were subsequently randomized
to vitamin E supplements,

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:05.580
the drug memantine, both,
or neither (just placebos)

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:06.420
for two years.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.360
Although there was no slowing
of cognitive decline

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.340
after adjusting
for multiple comparisons,

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.220
there was a slowing
of functional decline,

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.260
in terms of managing
daily activities

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.700
like bathing or dressing.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.520
The vitamin E group
appeared to only suffer

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.580
about 1.5 years
of functional deterioration

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:24.660
in the two years' time,

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:27.960
resulting in approximately
two hours less caregiver time

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:30.420
required each day
compared to the drug group.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.740
If those were the only two
clinical vitamin E trials,

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:37.860
I would give vitamin E
serious consideration

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.480
for those
with Alzheimer's disease.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:43.320
But, there's a third,
a six-month trial,

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:45.960
that suggested vitamin E
may be detrimental

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.180
to cognition
in some Alzheimer's patients.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.000
Obviously, more research
needs to be done.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.540
In my next video,
I'll cover other antioxidants

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.040
that have been put to the test,

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.380
including vitamin C,
beta carotene,

00:03:58.380 --> 00:03:59.880
Centrum multivitamin,

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.160
and the minerals
zinc and calcium.

